This study examines the fundamental particle physics characteristics of the minimal scoto-seesaw (MSS) model, with a specific focus on the vacuum stability of the Higgs potential and the dark matter (DM) phenomenology of the MSS. While previous investigations into MSS dark matter relied on numerical methods to explore the parameter space, we establish analytical constraints that efficiently delineate the viable dark matter co-annihilation regions. We find that at the LHC scale, the mass of the dominant dark matter co-annihilating particle is precisely determined by its annihilation efficiency and the seesaw scale. Specifically, for fine-tained MSS models with a light Higgs, the mass of the primary DM co-annihilator falls within the range of 300 GeV to 500 GeV. Furthermore, we demonstrate that for the MSS model to remain stable up to the Planck scale, the dark sector neutrino Yukawa coupling must remain small. Finally, we provide a detailed examination of the MSS model's phenomenology, particularly regarding its potential visibility at colliders and other unique physical properties. Complementary materials, including a detailed roadmap for navigating the MSS parameter space and an analysis of its beyond-Standard-Model effective field theory, are available at https://github.com/yangyiyi1993/MSS_.

